editorialMd:
Build the result list

Each row of drawers is a booking: [start, end, reward]. It uses time from start up to, but not including, end. A booking may start exactly when another one ends. Sort the bookings by end time, then start time, then reward, all from smallest to largest. Look at the first booking, then the first two bookings, and so on. For each of these groups, choose bookings that do not overlap and give the largest total reward. Record that reward. You may choose no bookings for a reward of 0. With no bookings, the result list is empty.

Return the answer

Read the result list from left to right. Return the first number that is at least k (greater than or equal to k). Return the number, not its position. If none matches, return -1.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function paidReservationsFirstQualifyingSlip(drawers, k) {
  const a=drawers; let out=[];
  const rows=a.map(r=>[...r]).sort((x,y)=>x[1]-y[1]||x[0]-y[0]||x[2]-y[2]),dp=[0];for(let i=0;i<rows.length;i++){let l=0,r=i;while(l<r){const m=(l+r)>>1;if(rows[m][1]<=rows[i][0])l=m+1;else r=m;}dp.push(Math.max(dp[i],dp[l]+rows[i][2]));}out=dp.slice(1);
  return out.find(x=>x>=k)??-1;
}


